    "note_tweet": {
      "entities": {},
      "text": "IMO Koreans learned this from Chinese immigrants, modified and adapted it for Korean flavour, so there exist some differences between Jajangmyeon and Zhajiangmian and I think it’s ok to be two similar but different dishes. \nSimilarly Wonton has a lot of variants inside China, Yuntun (the word that “Wonton” was translated from), Hundun, Chaoshou, Bianshi, with different names and a bit different shapes and cooking methods, but none feels like one of those was not “invented” at all and was actually stolen from another.\n\n(I’m not assuring that Korean people haven’t stolen anything from anyone, and I’m just talking about these dishes.)"
    },
